  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Continuous Frequency",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret continuous frequency as the variable that replaces discrete harmonic indices when the Fourier-series period grows without bound.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous Frequency",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Continuous Frequency",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a periodic signal with period $T$, the Fourier-series spectrum is not continuous. It sits on harmonic frequencies $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$, where $n$ is an integer label and $\\omega_0$ is the spacing between adjacent lines.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_n = n\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi n}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The spacing is the important part: adjacent harmonics differ by $\\Delta\\omega = \\omega_{n+1} - \\omega_n = \\omega_0$, and $\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$ ties that spacing directly to the period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta\\omega = \\omega_{n+1} - \\omega_n = \\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "As $T$ grows, $\\omega_0$ shrinks, so the same harmonic labels are packed more tightly on the frequency axis. The spectrum still uses integers $n$, but the frequency samples sit closer together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{T\\to\\infty} \\Delta\\omega = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the point of continuous frequency: when the grid becomes arbitrarily dense, the integer index stops being the natural coordinate, and the spectrum is better read with a continuous variable $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous Frequency",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying Discrete Harmonic Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has period $T = 4$ s. Find $\\omega_0$ and list the harmonic frequencies for $n = -2,-1,0,1,2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the period $T$, which tells us to use the Fourier-series harmonic grid. The spectrum is not arbitrary; it sits at frequencies $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$, with one line for each integer $n$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_n = n\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi n}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $T = 4$ s, the spacing is $\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ rad/s. That means each harmonic line is separated from the next one by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = \\frac{2\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now multiply $\\omega_0$ by the listed integer labels. The harmonic frequencies are $-\\pi$, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $0$, $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and $\\pi$ rad/s, written from negative to positive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\{-\\pi,\\,-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,\\pi\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a discrete Fourier-series grid: only the harmonic frequencies at those integer labels belong to the spectrum, and nothing sits between the lines.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous Frequency",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Seeing Frequency Samples Become Denser",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare periodic signals with periods $T = 2$ s and $T = 8$ s. Compute $\\omega_0$ for each and decide which spectrum has the denser harmonic grid over the same frequency interval.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a comparison of two periods, so use the same harmonic spacing rule from the previous step. For each signal, compute $\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$ and compare the line spacing on the Fourier-series grid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi,\\qquad \\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{8} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $T = 2$ s, the harmonic lines are $\\pi$ rad/s apart. For $T = 8$ s, they are only $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ rad/s apart, so the larger-period signal has much tighter spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta\\omega_{T=2} = \\pi,\\qquad \\Delta\\omega_{T=8} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That spacing is the gap between adjacent harmonic lines. Over the same frequency interval, the grid with the smaller gap fits more samples, so the $T = 8$ spectrum is denser than the $T = 2$ spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous Frequency",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Replacing Harmonic Indices with a Frequency Variable",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A Fourier-series spectrum is written with harmonics at $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$. Describe how the notation changes as $T \\to \\infty$ and $\\omega_0 \\to 0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the harmonic grid $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$. Here $n$ is only a discrete label, and $\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$ is the fixed spacing between adjacent lines.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_n = n\\omega_0,\\qquad \\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "As $T \\to \\infty$, the spacing collapses: $\\Delta\\omega = \\omega_{n+1} - \\omega_n = \\omega_0 \\to 0$. Once the gap vanishes, the integer index stops being the useful coordinate on the frequency axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta\\omega = \\omega_{n+1} - \\omega_n = \\omega_0 \\to 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The relabeling is the main point: replace the harmonic index $n$ with the continuous frequency variable $\\omega$. In the Fourier-transform setting, the spectrum is read on the $\\omega$ axis, not as a list of integer harmonic numbers.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous Frequency",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading a Spectrum on a Continuous Frequency Axis",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A spectrum is described as $X(\\omega)$ on a continuous frequency axis, and you are asked to read the value at $\\omega = 1.3\\pi$ rad/s. Explain how to interpret that request.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the continuous axis in $X(\\omega)$. Read $\\omega$ as the coordinate itself, so $\\omega = 1.3\\pi$ rad/s names a location on the spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega = 1.3\\pi\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In a Fourier series, you would be restricted to harmonic locations $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$. Here, the point is that $1.3\\pi$ does not have to equal an integer multiple of a spacing to be meaningful.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_n = n\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the correct reading is: take the spectrum value at that frequency coordinate. That is the bridge to Fourier-transform notation, where $\\omega$ is the axis label and not a harmonic index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
